const faqItems = [
  { question: "How many dispensaries are in Maine?", answer: "It depends on which count you mean. The OCP 2025 Annual Report counted 180 active adult-use retail stores as of December 31, 2025. The dated live OCP licensee roster records 106 deduplicated adult-use Store entries in 50 municipalities as of July 8, 2026. A separate June 1, 2026 canonical snapshot counted 187 distinct active Store-license identities. These are different definitions and dates, not contradictions — the annual report is a year-end total, the live roster is a point-in-time deduplicated count, and the license-identity snapshot counts distinct licenses rather than storefronts." },
  { question: "Why do different sources give different Maine dispensary counts?", answer: "Because they measure different things on different dates. The OCP 2025 Annual Report's 180 is a December 31, 2025 year-end total of active retail-store establishments. The live OCP roster's 106 (as of July 8, 2026) is a deduplicated point-in-time count of active Store entries, which excludes conditional approvals still pending and reflects normal license turnover. The 187 figure counts distinct Store-license identities in a June 1, 2026 snapshot. Always check the date and definition behind any single number." },
  { question: "How many Maine towns allow dispensaries?", answer: "As of OCP opt-in data refreshed July 17, 2026, 78 of Maine's 485 municipalities have recorded retail authorization. The remaining 407 have no recorded retail authorization. Note that a municipality can authorize retail without yet having an active store, so the authorized-town count (78) and the active-storefront count are different measures." },
  { question: "How many medical cannabis dispensaries are in Maine?", answer: "Maine's medical program operates through registered dispensaries and a large caregiver network. The dated live OCP roster records 269 deduplicated caregiver storefronts in 116 municipalities as of July 8, 2026, in addition to registered medical dispensaries. Medical patients can also purchase from registered adult-use stores that serve the medical market." },
  { question: "Is the number of Maine dispensaries growing or shrinking?", answer: "By dollars the market is flat-to-slightly-up; by active operator count it has tightened modestly. The active retail-store count moved from the 180 year-end 2025 annual-report peak to 106 deduplicated live-roster entries by July 8, 2026 — a gap that reflects normal license turnover (closures and conditional approvals still pending) rather than a market crash. Maine's 2026 adult-use market is on pace for roughly $244-248M, a plateau year." },
  { question: "Where are most Maine dispensaries located?", answer: "Adult-use sales and storefronts are heavily concentrated in southern Maine. York and Cumberland counties together account for nearly 60% of all Maine adult-use retail sales, and they host the densest cluster of stores. The remaining sales spread across the other 14 counties, with Penobscot (Bangor), Androscoggin (Lewiston-Auburn), and Kennebec (Augusta-Waterville) rounding out the top five." },
  { question: "How big is Maine's cannabis market overall?", answer: "Maine's adult-use market generated $246.4 million in retail sales across 4.84 million transactions in calendar year 2025, a 1.0% increase over 2024. Through the first half of 2026, sales totaled $119.95M, putting the state on pace for $244-248M for the full year. The market serves a state population of roughly 1.4 million plus significant cross-border demand from New Hampshire and Massachusetts." },
  { question: "How do I find a dispensary near me in Maine?", answer: "Use our dispensary finder to locate licensed stores by town, or browse our city-by-city dispensary guides for detailed coverage of each market. Because only 78 of 485 municipalities authorize retail, your nearest dispensary may be in a neighboring town — the finder shows what is actually open near you." }
